# Oroboros Protocol: design guardrails

## Core-rule protection (the owner asked for this reminder)

Never change a core game rule just to make ONE card/Daemon/item work.
Core rules: resolve exactly 3 Nodes per turn; room = 3 + Stash capacity; the unplayed
Node(s) become the Stash; 20 base Server Integrity; deal 4 / resolve 3 / stash 1; lose only at
0 Integrity or when the Tailbytes are exhausted without meeting the threshold.

Whenever the owner proposes a mechanic change motivated by a single card (for example
"make 4 nodes resolvable so Cloud Sync works"), STOP and remind them of this rule first,
then offer to rewrite the card's own text/behaviour instead. Card behaviour adapts to
the engine, not the other way round.

## Layout

See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. Logic is UI-free in scripts/logic, data in scripts/database.

## Terminology (fiction: you are an Operator; your OROBOROS PROTOCOL siphons bytes from a Server but can also destroy it)
Display names changed; code identifiers (`DECK`, `ICE`, `BREACH`, `COOLANT`, `health`, `chips`, `mult`...) did NOT.
- Deck -> **Tailbytes** | Data Cache node (`NodeType.DATA`) -> **Siphon** | ICE node (`NodeType.ICE`) -> **Glitch**
- Coolant node (`COOLANT`) -> **Patch** | Breach Protocol / weapon (`BREACH`) -> **Firewall**
- Rig Health -> **Server Integrity** | Data / score -> **bytes** (Base Data -> **Base Bytes**) | Bandwidth / mult -> **Overclock**
- Flee -> **Skip** | Black-ICE server -> **Sentinel** server
- Renamed to avoid clashes: Daemon "Glitch" -> Ghost Write (id `glitch`), Daemon "Firewall" -> Shock Absorber (id `firewall`),
  Subroutine "Data Dump" -> Bulk Siphon, Subroutine "Overclock" -> Full Spectrum, Executable "Overclock" -> Hyperdrive.
- Quota -> **Threshold** (code still uses `quota`)
